================================================================================
THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS UNDER OTHER TERMS
================================================================================

The MIT terms above cover the code written for this project, and since the x86
decoder was rewritten there is no third-party code in this repository. What
follows records what it was built from and with, because some of that is a debt
even where no licence attaches.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Previously used: bddisasm 3.0.1 - Apache License 2.0
    nothing of it remains in this repository
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Copyright (c) Bitdefender. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

Earlier versions of this project vendored bddisasm as the x86 and x86-64 decoder under the
emulator. It has been removed from the tree, and the decoder that replaced it,
`libgenome/genotype/x86/`, is a complete rewrite: a table-driven decoder with its
own format, its own runtime and its own text formatter, none of it derived from
bddisasm's source.

[Unverified] What this repository still holds that came from running bddisasm:
`libgenome/genotype/x86/x86_tab.c` and `x86_ids.h` are data written by
`tools/genotype/x86_gen.c`, which was run against bddisasm and recorded what it
answered - the opcode map, operand shapes and instruction identities, including
the numbering of the instruction ids. No bddisasm source file is in the tree and
the generator does not include one. Whether facts of that kind carry the source
licence is a legal question this file does not answer; it is recorded so that a
reader can decide, and the safe reading - that Apache 2.0's attribution applies -
is the one honoured here: the copyright above is kept and the licence text is at
`LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt`. Reproducing the tables needs a checkout of bddisasm
passed as `make genotype-x86 REF=<dir>`; it is not shipped.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Previously consulted: iced (MIT) and yaxpeax-x86 (0BSD)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Two Rust x86 decoders were read for how a fast decoder is organised - table
indexing by opcode map and prefix, decoding and operand building as separate
steps, validity decided by the table rather than by checks in the decoder. No
code, table or text from either is in this repository, and neither is a build
or run-time dependency. Their terms are recorded because the reading was real.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Capstone - BSD-3-Clause (some files NCSA): offline reference only
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Capstone is used on a developer's machine as the reference the ARM decoders'
tables are derived from, the way bddisasm was for x86. It is not distributed
with, linked into or required to build kofeng, and no Capstone source is
reproduced here. Its terms are noted for the same reason as above.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Signature sources - bases/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The detection signatures under `bases/` are covered by the terms above, the
same as the engine.

They used to carry a separate file saying the same words, kept apart so that
the terms on detection data could be changed later without reopening the
engine's. That option is given up here deliberately: a second licence file
stating identical terms is a thing readers have to diff to discover says
nothing new, and the separation it bought was never used. If the terms on
signature data ever do need to diverge, that is the point at which a file
saying so earns its place - and not before.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Projects read, from which no code is taken
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Nothing in this section is redistributed here and none of these terms attach to
this Software. The names are recorded because the debt is real even where the
licence does not follow it, and because a reader who recognises a technique
should be able to find where it came from.

    ClamAV (GPL-2.0)                the NRV2B/D/E codings
    Unlicense (GPL-3.0)             what an import table rebuild has to do
    Unipacker (GPL-2.0)             section hopping and write-then-execute
    RetDec (MIT)                    MPRESS's build table and fix stub, and
                                    separately its DECODER: candidate bytes
                                    tiered by section, a worklist of jump
                                    targets, each byte read once, a dry run
                                    before a guessed target is believed, and
                                    bounding the input instead of capping the
                                    results
    Capstone (BSD-3-Clause, with    not linked: 31 MB of binary against
      LLVM-derived files under      2.8 MB today, and its DIET build strips
      the NCSA licence)             the detail that made it worth having.
                                    Used offline as the reference the ARM
                                    decoders' tables are derived from
    Unpacker (MIT)                  the routing rule that named the above
    VMPStatic (MIT)                 VMProtect's PACKER_INFO and key schedule
    XVolkolak / XEmulUnpacker (MIT) nSpDelta, and the per-packer hand-over tails
    themida-unmutate (GPL-3.0)      Themida's mutation layer
    UnpackThemida (GPL-3.0)         the same, from the other direction
    themida-dumper (MIT)            the .themida/.winlice and .boot pair
    Themida-Research                the same
    VMProtectDumper (PolyForm       the dumping strategy. NONCOMMERCIAL terms:
      Noncommercial 1.0.0)          nothing is taken, and anyone who later wants
                                    to take something must read that licence
    OreansCrack (MIT)               VMProtect's virtual machine
    vmp2 (MIT)                      the same
    vid (MIT)                       the same
    UnSafengine64                   Safengine's shape
    OllyDbg and x64dbg scripts      per-build offsets, read as documentation

A LICENCE ATTACHES TO CODE, NOT TO A FACT. If any line of any of these were
reproduced here, that project's terms would apply and the remedy would be to
remove the line, not to reword this paragraph. THIRD-PARTY.md says, for each
one, exactly which fact was taken and what was written independently.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Test material
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`tests/fixtures/` and any sample corpus referenced by the test scripts are
inputs, not part of the Software. Where a fixture is generated it is covered by
the MIT terms above; where one is a third-party artefact it is not redistributed
here, and the scripts that want it say where to get it.
